      companyBM: 'Zava Bank Malaysia',
      taglineBM: 'Pinjaman terjejas kasar pada 6.8% dan perlindungan peruntukan pada 71% menjelang semakan dua hala ASEAN Central Bank.',
      scenarioBM: 'Zava Bank Malaysia ialah bank komersial Malaysia peringkat pertengahan yang tersenarai di ASEAN Stock Exchange, dengan kira-kira 7,200 pekerja, 138 cawangan, dan pinjaman kasar RM 74.6 bilion merentas bahagian Corporate Banking, SME, Consumer, Treasury, dan Special Assets. Dalam empat suku terakhir, pinjaman terjejas kasar meningkat daripada 4.1% kepada 6.8%, didorong tekanan dalam SME berkaitan pembinaan, pembiayaan kereta terpakai, dan beberapa akaun pengangkutan berleveraj, manakala baki Stage 2 berkembang kepada RM 8.9 bilion. Perlindungan peruntukan kini 71% berbanding lantai dalaman 80%, CET1 mengecil kepada 13.1%, dan 19 fasiliti peminjam dalam Covenant Monitor sudah berada dalam perbincangan waiver atau pelanggaran. Tab Credit Rating Watch menunjukkan 27 obligor diturunkan penarafan dalam 90 hari terakhir, termasuk 6 nama yang menyumbang lebih RM 420 juta pendedahan. ASEAN Central Bank telah menjadualkan semakan kualiti aset dua hala dalam lima hari dan mahukan naratif yang boleh dipertahankan tentang disiplin underwriting, aktiviti penstrukturan semula, dan management overlays. Pasukan Hubungan Pelabur juga menyediakan mesej sensitif ASEAN Stock Exchange kerana counterparty pembiayaan borong mula bertanya sama ada panduan FY2026 masih mengandaikan kos kredit ternormal.',
      companyIDEN: 'Zava Bank Indonesia',
      taglineIDEN: 'Gross non-performing loan ratio at 6.8% and 71% coverage ahead of the ASEAN Financial Services Authority review and ASEAN Stock Exchange investor scrutiny.',
      scenarioIDEN: 'Zava Bank Indonesia is a mid-tier Indonesian commercial bank listed on ASEAN Stock Exchange with around 7,200 employees, 138 branches, and IDR 74.6 trillion in gross loans across Corporate Banking, SME, Consumer, Treasury, and Special Assets divisions. Over the last 4 quarters, the non-performing loan ratio has risen from 4.1% to 6.8%, driven by stress in construction SMEs, used-vehicle financing, and several leveraged transport accounts, while Stage 2 balances have expanded to IDR 8.9 trillion. Provision coverage is now 71% against an internal floor of 80%, CET1 has narrowed to 13.1%, and 19 debtor facilities on the Covenant Monitor are already in waiver or breach discussions. The Credit Rating Watch tab shows 27 obligors downgraded in the last 90 days, including 6 names contributing more than IDR 420 billion of exposure. ASEAN Financial Services Authority has scheduled a bilateral asset-quality review in 5 days and wants a defensible narrative on underwriting discipline, restructuring activity, and management overlays. Investor Relations and Finance teams are preparing ASEAN Stock Exchange-sensitive messaging in parallel because wholesale funding counterparties have started questioning whether FY2026 guidance still assumes normalised credit costs.',
